WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:06.713
That night there was little change in our situation; we were still under five hundred feet of ice.

2
00:00:06.714 --> 00:00:14.713
During the night I woke up several times and found that we were slowly rising to the surface as the layer of ice above us grew thinner.

3
00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:20.285
Finally, at six o'clock in the morning, Captain Nemo came to the door of the gallery.

4
00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:26.364
"The sea is open," was all he said before quickly departing, but that was enough for me.

5
00:00:26.365 --> 00:00:33.872
I went up to the platform, where the captain was looking at the water; it was still crowded with pieces of ice and moving icebergs.

6
00:00:33.873 --> 00:00:36.602
"Are we at the South Pole?" I asked.

7
00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:42.523
"I don't know," he replied. "I'll take our bearings at noon to determine our location."

8
00:00:42.524 --> 00:00:50.182
Captain Nemo invited my companions and me to join him when he set foot on land, but only Conseil and I were interested.

9
00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:56.586
Two crew members rowed us to shore in the small boat. Conseil was about to jump out when I held him back.

10
00:00:56.587 --> 00:01:03.579
"Sir," I said to Captain Nemo, "the honor of being first to set foot on land belongs to you."

11
00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:10.571
"Thank you," said the captain. He climbed out of the boat, and after a few minutes said to us, "Whenever you like . .

12
00:01:10.572 --> 00:01:15.492
." We followed him onto the rocky shore, where we observed seals and

13
00:01:15.493 --> 00:01:23.254
penguins. Unfortunately, it was too foggy to see the sun and take our bearings, so we were still not sure about where we had

14
00:01:23.255 --> 00:01:28.397
landed. We returned to the submarine, disappointed but hoping to have better luck the next

15
00:01:28.398 --> 00:01:35.810
day. Unfortunately the following morning, our view of the sun was once again obscured, this time by heavy

16
00:01:35.811 --> 00:01:43.064
snow. Finally, on the third day, Captain Nemo led us toward a peak, where we watched the sun burst through the

17
00:01:43.065 --> 00:01:47.397
clouds. He made his observations and confirmed that we were at the South

18
00:01:47.398 --> 00:01:59.874
Pole. "I, Captain Nemo, have reached the South Pole today, March 21, 1868, and I take possession of this part of the globe," he said

19
00:01:59.875 --> 00:02:07.016
gravely. "In the name of which monarch?" I asked, remembering how an explorer usually claimed land on behalf of his

20
00:02:07.017 --> 00:02:13.945
ruler. "In my own name!" replied Captain Nemo, holding up a black flag bearing a gold

21
00:02:13.946 --> 00:02:19.865
N. The next morning, at six o'clock, the crew of the Nautilus prepared to leave the South Pole.

22
00:02:19.866 --> 00:02:25.349
With its reservoirs filling with water, the submarine began its slow descent.

23
00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:31.762
At one thousand feet below the surface, it headed north at a steady 15 miles per hour.

24
00:02:31.763 --> 00:02:36.008
Toward night it was already floating under an immense iceberg.

25
00:02:36.009 --> 00:02:40.270
At three o'clock in the morning, I was awakened by a violent shock.

26
00:02:40.271 --> 00:02:45.159
I sat up in bed and was thrown into the middle of the room by another shock.

27
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:50.151
The Nautilus , having struck something, had rebounded and hit something else.

28
00:02:50.152 --> 00:02:54.238
I rushed to the gallery, where I found the furniture overturned.

29
00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:59.325
I heard footsteps and excited voices, but Captain Nemo did not appear.

30
00:02:59.326 --> 00:03:03.063
As I was leaving the gallery, Ned and Conseil entered.

31
00:03:03.064 --> 00:03:05.650
"What's going on?" I asked them.

32
00:03:05.651 --> 00:03:09.905
"That's what I came to ask you, Master," said Conseil.

33
00:03:09.906 --> 00:03:13.881
Ned scowled. "The Nautilus has run aground. Judging

34
00:03:13.882 --> 00:03:19.548
from the way it's lying, I doubt it'll right itself as it did in the Torres Strait." "So,

35
00:03:19.549 --> 00:03:23.889
do you think we're at the surface?" asked Conseil. "That's

36
00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:25.898
easy enough to find out." I

37
00:03:25.899 --> 00:03:32.881
consulted the instruments, and to my great surprise, I discovered that we were more than a thousand feet underwater.

38
00:03:32.882 --> 00:03:35.286
"We must speak to Captain Nemo."

39
00:03:35.287 --> 00:03:41.627
The three of us went in search of the captain, but when we couldn't find him, I decided he must be in the pilothouse.

40
00:03:41.628 --> 00:03:44.357
We returned to the gallery to wait for him.

41
00:03:44.358 --> 00:03:49.699
After about twenty minutes, the captain entered the room, but he took no notice of us.

42
00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:53.691
His normally emotionless face showed signs of unease.

43
00:03:53.692 --> 00:03:58.040
He watched the compass silently and then read the other instruments.

44
00:03:58.041 --> 00:04:03.532
I didn't want to interrupt him, but when he turned toward me, I decided to speak.

45
00:04:03.533 --> 00:04:09.762
"An incident, Captain?" I asked, using the phrase he'd preferred in the Torres Strait.

46
00:04:09.763 --> 00:04:12.588
"No, Professor, it's an accident."

47
00:04:12.589 --> 00:04:14.175
"Serious?"'

48
00:04:14.176 --> 00:04:20.508
"Perhaps," he replied. "We've run aground underwater, but we aren't in immediate danger."

49
00:04:20.509 --> 00:04:24.183
"May I ask, sir, what caused this accident?"

50
00:04:24.184 --> 00:04:29.921
"An enormous block of ice, an entire iceberg, turned over and slid under the Nautilus ."

51
00:04:29.922 --> 00:04:37.723
Captain Nemo said that the submarine was now emptying its reservoirs in an attempt to right itself, but the block of ice was rising with it.

52
00:04:37.724 --> 00:04:41.739
He explained this while never taking his eyes from the instruments.

53
00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:48.913
Suddenly we felt a small movement below us; evidently the submarine was righting itself slightly.

54
00:04:48.914 --> 00:04:58.564
Ten long minutes passed while we watched the pictures on the wall slowly return to their normal position and the floor become horizontal under our feet.

55
00:04:58.565 --> 00:05:01.390
"Are we floating again?" I asked.

56
00:05:01.391 --> 00:05:05.795
"Of course," replied the captain, "but we're still underwater."

57
00:05:05.796 --> 00:05:13.636
I looked through the windows and saw that a dazzling wall of ice rose about ten yards away from either side of the Nautilus .

58
00:05:13.637 --> 00:05:16.803
There was also ice above and below us.

59
00:05:16.804 --> 00:05:21.628
We were imprisoned in a perfect tunnel of ice filled with water.

60
00:05:21.629 --> 00:05:24.874
But I decided it would be easy to escape the tunnel.

61
00:05:24.875 --> 00:05:30.128
The submarine could go either forward or backward and then dive under the iceberg.

62
00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:34.628
Captain Nemo left us, and I assumed he returned to the pilothouse.

63
00:05:34.629 --> 00:05:41.453
My companions and I stayed at the gallery windows, dazzled by the submarine's light sparkling on the ice.

64
00:05:41.454 --> 00:05:48.771
At five in the morning, we felt a shock at the vessel's bow; its battering ram had obviously struck a block of ice.

65
00:05:48.772 --> 00:05:55.596
"We must go in reverse now, and see if we can find another way out," I said, trying to appear

66
00:05:55.597 --> 00:06:01.612
cheerful. "That'll take hours more," grumbled Ned. "How long are we going to be stuck

67
00:06:01.613 --> 00:06:06.088
here?" I had no idea, so I went to the library to calm my

68
00:06:06.089 --> 00:06:09.342
nerves. I threw myself on a couch and tried to read a

69
00:06:09.343 --> 00:06:13.414
book. Fifteen minutes later Conseil and Ned entered the

70
00:06:13.415 --> 00:06:17.255
room. "Is that an interesting book, Master?" asked

71
00:06:17.256 --> 00:06:19.652
Conseil. "Very interesting," I

72
00:06:19.653 --> 00:06:24.239
replied. "Well, it should be." Conseil laughed. "It's the one you

73
00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:29.485
wrote!" I slammed the book shut, and the two of them turned to go. I stood

74
00:06:29.486 --> 00:06:34.993
up. "Let's return to the gallery," I said. "We should stay together until we're out of this

75
00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:38.152
predicament." "As you wish, Master," replied

76
00:06:38.153 --> 00:06:44.819
Conseil. Several hours passed while the Nautilus moved backward at a good rate of speed, and we kept an eye on the instruments.

77
00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:49.977
It was almost 8:30 when we felt another shock, this time at the stern.

78
00:06:49.978 --> 00:06:53.636
I turned pale and seized Conseil's hand.

79
00:06:53.637 --> 00:06:56.636
The three of us exchanged troubled glances.

80
00:06:56.637 --> 00:06:59.723
At this point Captain Nemo entered the room.

81
00:06:59.724 --> 00:07:04.721
"The iceberg has shifted and closed up every outlet," he announced.

82
00:07:04.722 --> 00:07:09.103
Once again we were trapped in the ice!

